                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_26\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit's message states that mCurPage should be set to null (updated to prevPage)\nimmediately when the last page is popped, even if a transition animation is going to play.\nThe fix diff moves `mContainerView.removeView(removedPage.getView());` and\n`mCurPage = prevPage;` out of `doFinalWorkForPopPageInternal` (which only runs after the\nanimation's postDelayed callback fires, or immediately if there's no animation) and hoists\nthem to execute unconditionally and immediately in `popPageInternal`, before the\nanimation-duration branch.\n\ncandidate_26 is exactly where this bug was introduced. Before this commit, the code set\n`mCurPage = prevPage;` immediately (right after `removedPage.onDetach()`), unconditionally,\nregardless of whether an animation would play afterward \u2014 i.e., the correct behavior. In\ncandidate_26, this logic was refactored: `mContainerView.removeView(...)`, `onDetach()`,\n`onHidden()`, and `mCurPage = prevPage` were all consolidated into a new\n`doFinalWorkForPopPageInternal(removedPage, prevPage)` method, which is invoked either inside\nthe `postDelayed` animation callback (i.e., only after the animation finishes) or immediately\nin the non-animated branch. This means that whenever a pop animation is playing,\n`mCurPage` is not updated to `prevPage` until the animation completes \u2014 reintroducing the\nexact stale-mCurPage bug that the fix commit later corrects by splitting the removeView/mCurPage\nassignment back out to run immediately, independent of the animation delay.\n\ncandidate_29 contains a diff textually identical to the fix commit itself (it reverts exactly\nwhat candidate_26 changed), confirming candidate_26 as the true bug-introducing commit and\ncandidate_29 as the fix being included among the candidates (not itself a plausible\nbug-introducing commit, since it corrects the bug rather than introduces it).\n"
